    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they have state-of-the-art courses by very \nprestigious partners like Google, IBM, Microsoft,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511680,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Meta. So, if you want to truly dive into the \nreal reason that the dollar is so dominant and how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518480,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it works, go subscribe to Coursera Plus and sign \nup for my recommended courses in the top comment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "below or in the description of this video. But \nfor now, for this video about why the petrodollar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system is the biggest myth in geopolitics, it's \nenough to know that the establishment of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eurodollar market had helped create the following \nsituation around the time of the petrodollar deal.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542320,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Around 1976, we can see that the US as part of \nofficial central bank reserves, the US dollar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550480,
    "dDurationMs": 9360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was absolutely dominating all other currencies, \nincluding the British pound. So the US dollar was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559840,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "already very much the dominant reserve currency. \nAnd the second part of the puzzle is that if we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look at the main reserve asset, US treasuries \nin the 1980s when it was actually at its peak,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Saudis had been buying a lot of them and also \nthe other Gulf countries. If we then look at US  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579120,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dollar treasuries, we can see that the Saudis and \nother Gulf countries really only held a very very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586480,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very very small part of treasuries. So they were \nactually not that important holders of US debt.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So crucially, the US dollar did not need the oil \nexporters. It was actually the other way around.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The oil exporters needed the US dollar because US \nfinancial markets were the only financial markets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the world that were easily able to absorb the \nbillions and billions that the Gulf countries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were earning from oil. In other words, having \nonly this tiny tiny fraction of US treasuries,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is great news if you are a Saudi central \nbanker or treasurer because it means that if you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get into trouble and you need to sell all of these \ndollars to wage a war or something like that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then you can just sell it all and the price of \nthe asset will not drop at all. On the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had you invested in British government debt, you \nmay have caused a massive British government debt  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crisis if there was a crisis in the Gulf and you \nhad to sell all of it. So yes, the 1974 US-Saudi  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "agreement changed the world of geopolitics by \nmaking the US the protector of the Gulf States and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651200,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the 1974 agreement also changed the global economy \nbecause it led to lower and more stable oil prices  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than would otherwise have been the case. So, \ntalking about oil-producing countries accepting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other currencies than the dollar being a threat \nto the reserve currency status of the US dollar,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670800,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that actually from the beginning on was always \nsimply wrong. But today it's even more ridiculous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678240,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than ever for two big reasons. Reason number \none, oil is actually far less important in our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685200,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economies today than it was in the 1970s. For \nexample, at the height of the 1979 oil crisis,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692320,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oil transactions accounted for about 13 \nto 15% of the American economy. But today,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699680,
    "dDurationMs": 9520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's only about 5 to 7% of US GDP. The reasons \nare simply that our economies got way more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
